<SiteLayout title="Solunar times · 3Dweather" path="/solunar-times" nav="guides"
  description="The major and minor solunar periods shown on station pages, and how they are worked out.">
  <article class="article">
    <h1>Solunar times</h1>
    <p class="lede">Periods of the day tied to the Moon's position, which many anglers plan around.</p>
    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p>Solunar tables were made popular by John Alden Knight in the 1920s and 1930s. The idea is that fish and
      game are more active when the Moon is overhead or underfoot, and to a lesser degree when it rises or sets. The
      evidence is mixed; we show the times because people use them, not as a promise of a good catch.</p>
    <h2>What the station pages show</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Major periods</strong>: two hours centred on the Moon passing overhead (its highest point in the
        sky) and underfoot (its lowest point, on the other side of the Earth).</li>
      <li><strong>Minor periods</strong>: one hour centred on moonrise and on moonset.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All times are worked out for the station's own position and time zone from the JPL DE421 ephemeris, the
      same data behind the sunrise, sunset and moon times on each page. On days when the Moon does not rise or set,
      there is no minor period for it.</p>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
